model {

  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // THE MACHINE — one command in, background workers out.
  // Every value here was read off CLI/beads-loop.sh, CLI/loop-log.sh, CLI/bin/bo,
  // hooks/worker-stop.sh, hooks/worker-fail.sh, hooks/hooks.json.
  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  boCli = cliTool "bo — the front door" {
    description '''
**What it is:** `CLI/bin/bo` — one command, `bo loop`, and nothing else. It finds the engine
next to itself (`../beads-loop.sh`), refuses to start where there is no beads DB
(`bd list --limit 1 --json` must answer an array), prints `bo: dispatching $PWD — Ctrl-C to stop.`,
then `bash`s the engine in the **foreground** so every tick is on screen.

`CLI/install-cli.sh` writes a **real launcher** onto PATH rather than a symlink, so the command
still reports a helpful error after the plugin is uninstalled.
'''
    link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/CLI/bin/bo "Source — CLI/bin/bo"
    link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/CLI/install-cli.sh "Source — CLI/install-cli.sh"
  }

  knobs = knobPanel "The run's parameters (env, read once at startup)" {
    description '''
| Knob | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `BO_MAX_PARALLEL` | `4` | workers spawned at once; the cap is recounted from `claude agents --json` every tick |
| `BO_POLL_S` | `3` | seconds between ticks; **must divide 60** or the run refuses to start, because the idle rate limit is `60 / BO_POLL_S` |
| `BO_MAX_TICKS` | `0` | stop after N ticks — a test seam only; 0 = endless |
| `BO_LOG_ROOT` | `~/.binary-obelisk/logs` | parent of the one timestamped dir this run writes |
| `BO_ALLOWED_LABELS` | unset or `*` = the whole AGENT set | a comma list narrows which labels may dispatch; a name the map does not hold matches nothing and only starves the frontier |

Validation before the first tick, each one fatal (`exit 1`): the four tools `bd jq claude git`
are on PATH; the three numeric knobs are numbers; `BO_POLL_S` divides 60; a beads DB resolves from
`$PWD` (`bd` walks **up**, so `BEADS_DIR` does not save you).
'''
    link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/CLI/beads-loop.sh#L68-L138 "Source — beads-loop.sh lines 68–138"
  }

  dispatcher = dispatcher "beads-loop.sh — the dispatcher" {
    description '''
**What it is:** the engine `bo loop` runs. A `while true` loop: `tick`, `sleep $BO_POLL_S`, again.
It is the only thing in the system that claims, dispatches, publishes the epic branch, or owns folders.

One tick, in the order the code runs it (`tick()`, line 436):

| # | Step | Reads | On failure |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | `branchesCurrentlyAtWork` — the set of branches already at work, read **before** claiming so the ticket in hand is never counted against itself | `bd list --status in_progress --limit 0 --json` → `.[] \| .metadata.branch \| sort -u` | — |
| 1 | capacity check | `claude agents --json`, background sessions whose `cwd` starts with `<tower>/.claude/worktrees` or equals the tower | waiting `max_parallel` / `max_parallel_stalled` |
| 2 | **claim** | `bd ready --claim --json` — atomic: assignee + `in_progress` + a 5-minute lease | `bd-failure`; an empty answer is a waiting line, not an error |
| 3 | match labels against the `AGENT` map — **exactly one** may match | `.[0].labels` | `label-ambiguous`, left claimed |
| 4 | read the branch | `.[0].metadata.branch` | `metadata-missing`, left claimed |
| 5 | one pair of hands per branch | is `branch` in the busy set? | `bd unclaim`, try the next claim, at most `BO_MAX_PARALLEL + 2` attempts a tick |
| 6 | find the PR base | `.[0].parent` → `epicGateOf` (≤ 4 hops of `bd show --json` `parent`) → the gate's own `branch`; `main` when the claim's parent **is** that topmost gate | `epic-pr-failed step=walk` |
| 7 | publish the epic branch + PR if the gate has none | `ensureEpicGatePr <gate-id>` | `epic-pr-failed step=…`, left claimed |
| 8 | make the branch's folder | `ensureWorktree branch prTarget` | `worktree-failed`, left claimed |
| 9 | spawn | `claude --bg --agent <agent> --permission-mode bypassPermissions`, cwd **inside** the worktree | `worker-not-dispatched`, `bd unclaim` |
| 10 | one log line, move on | `logTo claims "reason=dispatched"` | — |

**What it never does:** it never polls a worker, never reads worker output, never reaps, never
merges, never ends itself, never closes a ticket, and never derives an agent name from a label.
'''
    link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/CLI/beads-loop.sh "Source — CLI/beads-loop.sh"
    link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/.claude/binary-obelisk-v1-design.yaml "Source — the design model"
  }

  agentMap = noteDoc "The AGENT map — the only label→worker binding" {
    description '''
```bash
declare -A AGENT=(
    [backend-logic]=engineer      [frontend-ui]=frontend
    [unit-tests]=tester-unit      [feature-test]=tester-feature
    [epic-e2e-test]=tester-e2e    [feature-review]=review
    [epic-review]=review          [epic-merge-pr]=merge
)
```
The keys **are** the closed label set: `isLabel` requires the label to be in this map *and* in the
allowed set. Zero or two matches on a claim is the tower's bug, not the loop's — it never guesses a
specialist. `review` answers two labels; the three test labels are three **tiers**, and the label —
never `--agent` — is what carries the tier, so a mis-labelled ticket gets the wrong depth of
coverage and no worker can tell you it did. There is no `feature-merge` label: a feature IS a branch,
so there is nothing to assemble.
'''
    link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/CLI/beads-loop.sh#L78-L107 "Source — beads-loop.sh lines 78–107"
  }

  spawnEnv = knobPanel "BO_* — the environment the spawn carries" {
    description '''
Set on the `claude --bg` line and nothing else: this is how the hooks know which ticket to finish
and what to say about it. Without them the worker just stops and its ticket sits `in_progress`
until the lease lapses.

| Var | Value | Who reads it |
|---|---|---|
| `BO_TICKET` | `RECIPE-6` | both hooks — and it is the **only** test that tells a dispatched worker from any other session in any other project |
| `BO_BRANCH` | `feature/add-recipe` | Stop hook: commit here, push here, refuse if `git branch --show-current` disagrees |
| `BO_PR_TARGET` | `epic/recipe-box`, or `main` at the epic tier | Stop hook: the base of this branch's PR; never stored on the ticket |
| `BO_AGENT` | `engineer` | both hooks, so an alarm names who was doing the work |
| `BO_LABEL` | `backend-logic` | both hooks, so an alarm names the label that chose that worker |
| `BO_PROJECT_DIR` | the tower root, where `.beads` lives | both hooks — **the worktree is not the tower** |
| `BO_LOG_DIR` | this run's log dir | both hooks: alarms land in `$BO_LOG_DIR/errors` |
| `BO_DONE_FILE` | `$BO_LOG_DIR/done-<ticket with / to +>` | the worker touches it; the Stop hook is fail-closed on it |

`BO_LOG_QUIET=1` is set by the hooks before they source the line format: a hook must never print
into a session. The dispatcher leaves it unset, so its own lines also go to the terminal.
'''
    link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/CLI/beads-loop.sh#L396-L431 "Source — beads-loop.sh lines 396–431"
  }

  lineDef = logFormat "loop-log.sh — the one definition of a log line" {
    description '''
Sourced by the engine **and** by both hooks: one format for the whole system, or the files cannot be
read against each other.

```
<TAG padded to 8> <ISO time>\trun=… repo=… ticket=… label=… agent=… branch=… target=… session=… worktree=… <detail>
```

The nine keys are the whole contract and they are on **every line of every file**, `"-"` for whatever
is not known at that moment — an absent field would be indistinguishable from a field nobody set, and
an error line is worth nothing if it cannot say what it happened to. That shared key set is what makes
the files **JOIN**: an `errors` line names the ticket, label, agent, branch, PR target, repo and session,
and the same keys lead from it into `claims`, `closes` or `failures` by `grep` alone.

- `boIdent <key> <value>` carries a value on every following line; `boClearIdentity` wipes all but
  `run` and `repo` at the top of every tick, so no line inherits a ticket from the tick before.
- Anything outside the nine keys — a `step=`, a `gate=`, an exit code, `msg=` — is passed as **detail**
  to `logTo`. The key set is closed, or the joining stops working.
- The first argument of `logTo` is both the display tag and the file it appends to, so a line can never
  land in a file nobody watches.
- `session=` is the id `claude --bg` prints (`backgrounded · 287d3c4c`), read out of that worker's own
  `spawn-<ticket>` file by `sessionFromSpawn` — the id a human needs to `claude attach`, read, or stop.
'''
    link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/CLI/loop-log.sh "Source — CLI/loop-log.sh"
  }

  logRun = logStore "~/.binary-obelisk/logs/<date>_<time> — one dir per run" {
    description '''
**What it is:** the binary obelisk is the central home for everything a run leaves behind. The engine
creates **every** file up front (`claims errors waiting results closes failures`), so a missing file and
an empty file look the same and neither hides a silent failure — `closes` and `failures` are written by
the hooks, possibly long after the run ended.

A real line, all nine keys populated:

```
CLAIMS   2026-11-04T09:12:03	run=2026-11-04_09-12-01 repo=/home/dev/recipe-box ticket=RECIPE-6 label=backend-logic agent=engineer branch=feature/add-recipe target=epic/recipe-box session=287d3c4c worktree=/home/dev/recipe-box/.claude/worktrees/feature+add-recipe reason=dispatched
```
'''
    fClaims = logFile "claims" {
      description '''
Every ticket taken, and every one put back. `reason=dispatched` on a spawn, `reason=branch-busy unclaimed`
on a put-back, and `gate=… reason=epic-published gateBranch=… pr=…` when the loop publishes the epic branch —
that one line names no ticket of its own, because the identity the tick already carries is on it.
'''
    }
    fErrors = logFile "errors" {
      description '''
The alarm: every failure, whoever saw it. **Never rate-limited** — a waiting line is capped at one a minute
per reason, an error never is.
'''
    }
    fWaiting = logFile "waiting" {
      description '''
The quiet ticks, **one a minute per reason** (`60 / BO_POLL_S` ticks), each carrying
`working=<busy sessions>` and `next=<the id a bare bd ready would name>`.
'''
    }
    fCloses = logFile "closes" {
      description '''
The Stop hook's proof of work: one line per ticket that landed, `url=<the PR>`.
'''
    }
    fFailures = logFile "failures" {
      description '''
The StopFailure hook's account of a session that died mid-turn: `from=<state> kind=<.error> msg="<last assistant message>"`.
'''
    }
    fResults = logFile "results" {
      description '''
The run's tally — `ticks=N claimed=M` — at `BO_MAX_TICKS`, or on Ctrl-C (`onInterrupt`).
'''
    }
    fSpawn = logFile "spawn-<ticket>" {
      description '''
That one worker's raw spawn output, which holds its session id. It used to be one shared `last-spawn`
that every spawn overwrote, so the id survived only for the run's final worker — the one thing a human
needs to attach to, read, or stop.
'''
    }
    fDone = logFile "done-<ticket>" {
      description '''
The worker's own declaration that its brief is complete, created by `touch "$BO_DONE_FILE"` as its very
last action. The engine `rm -f`s it before spawning; the Stop hook refuses to land anything without it.
'''
    }
  }

  stopHook = hook "worker-stop.sh — the Stop hook, fail-closed" {
    description '''
**What it is:** the closer. Registered in `hooks/hooks.json` (`timeout: 300`), so it fires in **every
session of every project** the plugin is enabled in — `BO_TICKET` is what tells a dispatched worker
apart from everything else, and a session the loop did not spawn leaves on the first line.

```
worker says it is done → commit onto its feature's branch → push → the branch's PR →
write the PR url onto the ticket's pr metadata → close it
```

Refusals, in the order the code checks them, each one `exit 0` with the ticket untouched:
`missing-tool` → `ticket-not-in-work` (a second Stop in a closed session leaves silently) →
`no-branch` → `no-pr-target` → **`not-declared-done`** → `wrong-branch` → `ticket-has-no-title` →
`commit-failed` → `push-failed` → `forge-unknown` / `missing-tool` → `pr-not-created` →
`ticket-not-annotated` → `close-failed`.

**One branch, one pull request, forever:** the branch's first task creates the PR, and every later task
finds it by `gh pr list --head <branch> --base <target> --state open` and reuses it — which is why the
branch, not the ticket, is the identity of the work.
'''
    link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/hooks/worker-stop.sh "Source — hooks/worker-stop.sh"
  }

  failHook = hook "worker-fail.sh — the StopFailure hook" {
    description '''
**What it is:** what happens when a worker's session dies on an API error mid-turn (`timeout: 60`). A dead
worker stops heartbeating, its lease lapses, and `bd reclaim` would silently sweep the ticket back to
`open` for another attempt. This hook takes it out of that path **on purpose**: the ticket lands somewhere
a human will see it, with the reason attached, and stays until they say otherwise.

```bash
bd update "$ticket" --append-notes "Worker session <id> failed: <kind>
Last message: <last assistant message>"
bd update "$ticket" --status failed-agent
logTo failures "from=<state> kind=<kind> msg=\"…\""
```

The failure text is **never polled, parsed out of a log, or asked of the model**: the harness fires this
hook itself and hands the reason on stdin — `.error` is its coarse label for the kind of death (often just
`unknown`), `.last_assistant_message` is the last thing the session produced, which is where the real text
is (for local inference, typically an `API Error:` line from the proxy). Every step is *attempted, never
forced*: one that fails leaves the ticket exactly where it was and logs why.
'''
    link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/hooks/worker-fail.sh "Source — hooks/worker-fail.sh"
  }

  hooksJson = noteDoc "hooks/hooks.json — the registration" {
    description '''
Two hook events, both plain commands:

| Event | Command | Timeout |
|---|---|---|
| `Stop` | `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/worker-stop.sh` | 300 s |
| `StopFailure` | `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/worker-fail.sh` | 60 s |

Because the plugin registers them globally, both scripts start with `[[ -n "${BO_TICKET:-}" ]] || exit 0`.
That one line is what keeps the obelisk from committing your everyday sessions.
'''
    link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/hooks/hooks.json "Source — hooks/hooks.json"
  }

  beads = beadsStore "beads DB (bd) — the tower" {
    description '''
**What it is:** the loop's **only runtime input**. None of the design docs or CLAUDE.md files are read at
runtime; the graph — every gate, ticket, `depends-on` edge, and the two metadata keys — lives here.

The verbs the tower actually uses:

| Command | Why this one |
|---|---|
| `bd ready --claim --json` | the **only** sanctioned claim: atomic assignee + `in_progress` + 5-minute lease. A bare `bd ready` is not atomic and would let two loops steal the same ticket |
| `bd ready --json` (peek) | used once, inside `waiting()`, purely to name `next=` |
| `bd unclaim <id>` | put a claim back — branch already at work, or the spawn died |
| `bd heartbeat <id>` | the worker keeps its lease alive; it never closes its own ticket |
| `bd list --status in_progress --limit 0 --json` | the branches currently at work |
| `bd list --status open,blocked --limit 0 --json` | the probe that tells `frontier_empty` from `tower_stalled` |
| `bd show --json <id>` | walk the parent chain; verify a plan round trip |
| `bd update <id> --set-metadata k=v` | **merges** — which is why writing `pr` never disturbs the plan's `branch` |
| `bd close <id> --session <id> --reason …` | only the Stop hook, only on the done marker |
| `bd create -t gate -l <label> --parent <id>` | the plan agents' build |
| `bd where` | which DB a directory resolves — `bd` walks **up** and ignores `BEADS_DIR` |

Types are `gate` and `task`, and `gate` must be registered (`bd config set types.custom "gate"`):
`bd` does not reject an unregistered type, it **accepts and hides it**, so a broken gate and a working
one look identical. Check `bd types` before you trust one.
'''
    link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/skills/initialize-beads/SKILL.md "Source — the initialize-beads skill"
  }

  forge = forge "origin — GitHub or GitLab" {
    description '''
**What it is:** the forge the tower publishes to. `main` at the bottom, one PR per branch above it:
`feature/<slug>` → PR → the epic branch, `epic/<slug>` → PR → `main`.

**Which forge it is, is settled three ways, in this order** (`detectForge`, and the same three steps in
the Stop hook): an honest host name in the remote URL (`*gitlab*`, `*github*`); then the refs that host
actually publishes, because only GitHub serves `refs/pull/` and only GitLab serves
`refs/merge-requests|pipelines|environments/`; then a machine holding **exactly one** of `gh`/`glab`.
Where all three are ambiguous the hook refuses to guess and logs `forge-unknown` — a wrong guess pushes
a branch to the wrong forge.
'''
  }

  claudeCli = cliTool "claude — background sessions" {
    description '''
Two uses, both by the dispatcher:

- `claude --bg --agent <agent> --permission-mode bypassPermissions "<the six rules>"`, run with `cd`
  **inside** the branch's worktree, and deliberately **no `-w`** — `claude --worktree` only ever
  *creates* one, and this branch already owns its own.
- `claude agents --json` — the capacity count, recounted fresh every tick: background sessions whose
  `cwd` starts with `<tower>/.claude/worktrees` or equals the tower.

Workers run `bypassPermissions` unattended, so this loop only ever belongs in a repo you fully trust.
'''
  }

  gitCli = cliTool "git — worktrees, fetch, push" {
    description '''
The loop owns the folders now, because a worktree belongs to a **branch**, not to a ticket.

- `branchAlreadyCheckedOut`: parse `git worktree list --porcelain` for `refs/heads/<branch>` — a branch
  already checked out somewhere is reused, never re-added.
- `ensureWorktree <branch> <prTarget>` → `git worktree add -B <branch> <path> <start>`, under
  `<tower>/.claude/worktrees/`, where the folder name is the branch name with `/` → `+`
  (`feature/add-recipe` → `feature+add-recipe`).
- `<start>` is the **first** of `origin/<branch>`, `origin/<prTarget>`, `<prTarget>`, `HEAD` that resolves —
  a feature branch starts where it is going to land.
- `git -C <tower> fetch --quiet origin` before each lookup; `push -u origin <branch>` is the Stop hook's.
'''
  }

  forgeCli = cliTool "gh / glab" {
    description '''
Whichever `detectForge` settled on. Both are used the same way: list the PR already on this branch pair,
and create one only when there is none. Only a URL matching `/(pull|merge_requests)/[0-9]+` counts as a
pull request — an error message quoting a docs URL is not one.
'''
  }

  human = human "Human (Brodie)" {
    description '''
**What it is:** the only one who closes gates. Workers leave their tickets open and report in their final
message; the human reads the report, verifies the branch, and closes. There is **no `human` agent** — the
person enters through gates and closes them, and nothing is ever spawned for them. A claimable ticket
assigned to a human is a model bug.
'''
  }

  decisions = noteDoc "The judgments the machine never makes" {
    description '''
Everything else is mechanical. These are not:

- **What the epic is** — one epic, one `epic/<slug>` of 2–4 lowercase words saying what the thing *is*.
- **What counts as a feature** — a complete user-facing flow; phased foundations → core → dependent → polish.
- **Order inside a feature's line** — the logic, then its tests, then the UI. Never a fan.
- **The tier of a test ticket** — the label carries the tier, never `--agent`.
- **Whether the work is done** — the human verifies and closes; nothing self-closes, and the Stop hook only
  ever files a *task* on the worker's own declaration.
- **Merge conflicts between feature branches** — the `merge` agent resolves them; it writes no code and
  never merges into `main`.
'''
    link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/.claude/binary-obelisk-v1-design.yaml "Source — the design model"
  }

  tiersNote = noteDoc "The three names — category, label, agent" {
    description '''
| Name | What it is | Examples |
|---|---|---|
| **category** | a display hint in `bd list`; names a plan-task template | `backend-logic`, `frontend-ux`, `unit-tests`, `feature-test`, `epic-e2e-test`, `review`, `merge` |
| **label** | a string attached with `bd create -l`; a ticket may carry any number, but **exactly one** must be a key of the `AGENT` map | `backend-logic`, `frontend-ui`, `unit-tests`, `feature-test`, `epic-e2e-test`, `feature-review`, `epic-review`, `epic-merge-pr` |
| **agent** | a real file in `agents/` | `engineer`, `frontend`, `tester-unit`, `tester-feature`, `tester-e2e`, `review`, `merge` |

A task may list two categories; a ticket has exactly one **dispatching** label, and it alone decides the
worker. For the three test tiers the label, the category and the node key deliberately collapse to one
string, while the agent carries its own name — `unit-tests` → `tester-unit` (micro), `feature-test` →
`tester-feature` (composition), `epic-e2e-test` → `tester-e2e` (broad). A tier is named for the **test**,
never the tester and never the framework.

**Tiers vs tools:** a test agent owns a tier (what to cover, how deep) and is deliberately agnostic about
the framework; a `framework-*` skill owns the tool (syntax, config, run and debug). That is why no ticket
description carries framework syntax, and why the feature and epic tiers load the *same* skill — they differ
in scope, not in spelling.
'''
  }

  branchRules = noteDoc "Branches and the two metadata keys" {
    description '''
One branch per epic, one per feature; **a task is a commit on its feature's branch**. No branch per ticket,
no branch per worker.

| Fact | Where it lives | Who reads it |
|---|---|---|
| `branch` | the ticket's `metadata`, written by the `plan-*` agents (the ticket copies its feature's value **verbatim**) | the loop reads `.[0].metadata.branch` off the claim and hands it down as `BO_BRANCH` — nothing re-derives it and nothing parses text |
| `pr` | the same `metadata`, written by the loop (onto the epic gate) or the Stop hook (onto any ticket whose work landed) | a human, and the hook when it looks for the PR already on a branch |
| the PR's **base** | **nowhere on a ticket** — it is the epic gate's own `branch`, named by the parent chain | `tick()` walks `.[0].parent` (`epicGateOf`, two hops at most) and reads it with `readFieldOf`, handing it down as `BO_PR_TARGET` |

A copy of the base could drift from the gate it copies and nothing compared the two — the loop published
one while the hook aimed at the other. So the copy is gone. A colliding sibling branch gets **different
words**, never `-2`. The epic branch has **no other publisher**: nothing but the loop ever pushes
`epic/<slug>` or opens its PR into `main`.
'''
  }

  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // THE WORKERS — the spokes. Each is one agent file, dispatched by one label.
  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  workers = layer "The workers" {
    engineer = worker "engineer" {
      description '''
**Label `backend-logic` → `engineer`.** Business logic, endpoints, hooks, types, error factories.
Never writes tests, JSX or CSS. Respawns: a fresh session of the same agent re-checks the work inside
the one ticket before it may close.
'''
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/agents/engineer.md "Source — agents/engineer.md"
    }
    testerUnit = worker "tester-unit" {
      description '''
**Label `unit-tests` → `tester-unit` — the micro tier.** One back-end function alone; every branch, every
boundary, empty/null, every error it throws. Never a component: the UI is proved by the composition tier.
Waits on the back-end ticket it tests. Skill: `framework-jest`.
'''
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/agents/tester-unit.md "Source — agents/tester-unit.md"
    }
    frontend = worker "frontend" {
      description '''
**Label `frontend-ui` → `frontend`.** Components, layout, styling, the shared component system. Last in the
line, built against functions already proved. Its landing is what unblocks `feature-test`.
'''
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/agents/frontend.md "Source — agents/frontend.md"
    }
    testerFeature = worker "tester-feature" {
      description '''
**Label `feature-test` → `tester-feature` — the composition tier.** One feature's pieces proved wired; its
errors and successes surface. Never re-runs the micro tier's edge matrix, never walks cross-feature journeys.
Skill: `framework-playwright`.
'''
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/agents/tester-feature.md "Source — agents/tester-feature.md"
    }
    testerE2e = worker "tester-e2e" {
      description '''
**Label `epic-e2e-test` → `tester-e2e` — the broad tier.** Journeys across features on the real stack:
cross-feature state handoffs, session continuity, one success and one failure journey per feature. The least
verbose tier. Skill: `framework-playwright`.
'''
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/agents/tester-e2e.md "Source — agents/tester-e2e.md"
    }
    reviewAgent = worker "review" {
      description '''
**Labels `feature-review` and `epic-review` → `review`.** Three questions from first principles: does the code
do what its source of truth demands, do the tests prove it, is it simple and readable. Maps the system the
change sits in (entry → exit, following imports), instruments it with trace logs, runs the tests against the
instrumented code, posts findings as PR comments. Files no tickets and commits or pushes nothing.
Skill: `pr-comments`.
'''
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/agents/review.md "Source — agents/review.md"
    }
    mergeAgent = worker "merge" {
      description '''
**Label `epic-merge-pr` → `merge`.** Epic tier only. Reads the epic's finished feature branches off the feature
gates, assembles them onto the epic branch in its own worktree, resolves the conflicts between them, runs the
tests, reports. Writes no code and no tests; **never merges into `main`, never opens a PR** — the thumbs-up on
that PR is the human's.
'''
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/agents/merge.md "Source — agents/merge.md"
    }
  }

  planAgents = layer "The plan agents (outside the tower)" {
    planEpic = planner "plan-epic" {
      description '''
Vague idea → clarifying questions → research → **exactly one** epic gate plus its `epic-merge-pr`,
`epic-e2e-test` and `epic-review` tickets. Writes one metadata key — `branch: epic/<slug>` — onto the gate and
its three tickets. Builds the tower and does no work in it.
'''
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/agents/plan-epic.md "Source — agents/plan-epic.md"
    }
    planFeature = planner "plan-feature" {
      description '''
Epic → feature-sized features (complete user-facing flows), `parent-child` to the epic, phased foundations →
core → dependent → polish; each gets its own `feature/<slug>`, its `feature-test` and its `feature-review`.
An orphaned feature (no epic) aims its PR at `main`.
'''
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/agents/plan-feature.md "Source — agents/plan-feature.md"
    }
    planTask = planner "plan-task" {
      description '''
One feature → its tasks, each `parent-child` to the feature, **labelled at creation** with `bd create -l <label>`,
the feature's `branch` copied **verbatim** (a plan agent never invents a branch below its own tier), the tasks
chained onto their predecessor, and every ticket verified with a `bd show --json` round trip.
'''
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/agents/plan-task.md "Source — agents/plan-task.md"
    }
  }

  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // THE TOWER — a worked example. The machine on the left is verified against
  // source; this tower is an INVENTED example (a recipe box) used so the
  // walkthroughs can name real ticket ids, branches and log lines.
  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  tower = layer "The tower — worked example: a recipe box (invented ids, real shapes)" {
    epic = gate "Epic gate — Recipe Box · RECIPE-1" {
      description '''
**What it is:** the epic gate — the *what*; the human closes it once, after `epic-review`.

```
type:     gate                       (custom type: bd config set types.custom "gate")
branch:   epic/recipe-box            ← plan-epic's key, the only metadata it writes
pr:       https://github.com/…/pull/1 ← written by the LOOP, by ensureEpicGatePr
waits on: RECIPE-4 (epic-review)      — an explicit blocked-by edge onto the gate itself
```

A gate does **not** block its own children: they are claimable while it is open. What holds the next tier off
the frontier is that explicit edge, and only a human closes it — so the tower stalls here until someone verifies.
`bd ready` never shows a gate: it is structurally invisible, so there is nothing to exclude.
'''
      epicMergePr = ticket "epic-merge-pr · RECIPE-2" {
        description '''
```
title:    assemble the feature branches onto epic/recipe-box
label:    epic-merge-pr        → AGENT → merge
parent:   RECIPE-1             (the epic gate)
metadata: { "branch": "epic/recipe-box" }
depends:  blocked-by RECIPE-5, RECIPE-11 — the two feature gates
```
**Brief:** read the feature branches off the feature gates, assemble them onto `epic/recipe-box` in your own
worktree, resolve the conflicts between them, run the tests, report. No code, no tests, no merge into `main`,
no PR.
'''
      }
      epicE2e = ticket "epic-e2e-test · RECIPE-3" {
        description '''
```
title:    journeys across the whole app in a real browser
label:    epic-e2e-test        → AGENT → tester-e2e   (respawn: true)
parent:   RECIPE-1
metadata: { "branch": "epic/recipe-box" }
depends:  blocked-by RECIPE-2
skill:    framework-playwright
```
**Brief:** cross-feature state handoffs, session continuity, one success and one failure journey per feature.
Never re-test the micro tier's edges or the composition tier's wiring.
'''
      }
      epicReview = ticket "epic-review · RECIPE-4" {
        description '''
```
title:    the three questions over the whole epic
label:    epic-review          → AGENT → review        (respawn: true)
parent:   RECIPE-1
metadata: { "branch": "epic/recipe-box" }
depends:  blocked-by RECIPE-3
skill:    pr-comments
```
**Brief:** map entry → exit, instrument with trace logs, run the tests against the instrumented code, post
findings as PR comments. Then the human verifies and closes RECIPE-1.
'''
      }
    }

    featA = gate "Feature gate — Add a Recipe · RECIPE-5" {
      description '''
```
type:     gate
branch:   feature/add-recipe           ← plan-feature's key
pr:       https://github.com/…/pull/2  ← written by the Stop hook onto the ticket that first landed work
waits on: RECIPE-10 (feature-review)
PR base:  epic/recipe-box              ← read off the epic gate, never copied down
```
One complete user-facing flow: a user writes a recipe and sees it in the box. Its five tasks are **one linked
line, never a fan**, so the frontier can only ever hand out one task per feature and this branch is never
written by two workers at once.
'''
      backendA = ticket "backend-logic · RECIPE-6" {
        description '''
```
title:    validateRecipe + saveRecipe — pure functions
label:    backend-logic        → AGENT → engineer
category: backend-logic
parent:   RECIPE-5
metadata: { "branch": "feature/add-recipe" }   ← copied VERBATIM from the feature gate
depends:  none — the first task; with RECIPE-12 it IS the frontier, and both are claimable together
```
**Requirements**
1. `validateRecipe(input): Either<RecipeValidationError, Recipe>` — pure, no mutation
2. `saveRecipe(validated)` through the repository port only
3. reject: empty title, title > 120 chars, duplicate slug, missing ingredients

**Files:** `src/recipes/validate-recipe.ts`, `src/recipes/types.ts`, `src/recipes/ports.ts`
**Done means:** report in the final message; the Stop hook files it, not you.
'''
      }
      unitA = ticket "unit-tests · RECIPE-7" {
        description '''
```
title:    exhaustive coverage for validateRecipe
label:    unit-tests           → AGENT → tester-unit   (respawn: true)
category: unit-tests
parent:   RECIPE-5
metadata: { "branch": "feature/add-recipe" }
depends:  blocked-by RECIPE-6 — the back-end ticket it tests
skill:    framework-jest
```
**Cover:** every branch, every boundary (120/121 chars), empty and null input, each error it throws, the empty
ingredients list.
**Owned elsewhere:** composition wiring (feature tier), components (never here), journeys (epic tier).
'''
      }
      frontendA = ticket "frontend-ui · RECIPE-8" {
        description '''
```
title:    RecipeForm + RecipeCard against proved functions
label:    frontend-ui          → AGENT → frontend
category: frontend-ux
parent:   RECIPE-5
metadata: { "branch": "feature/add-recipe" }
depends:  blocked-by RECIPE-7
```
**Brief:** the form, validation feedback surfacing each `RecipeValidationError`, the card in the box. Layout and
styling only — the logic is already proved by the micro tier. This ticket landing is what unblocks `feature-test`.
'''
      }
      featureTestA = ticket "feature-test · RECIPE-9" {
        description '''
```
title:    the add-recipe pieces are wired
label:    feature-test         → AGENT → tester-feature (respawn: true)
category: feature-test
parent:   RECIPE-5
metadata: { "branch": "feature/add-recipe" }
depends:  blocked-by RECIPE-8 — the LAST task; nothing is proved against half a feature
skill:    framework-playwright
```
**Cover:** the composed flow saves a recipe; each encapsulated error surfaces to the user.
'''
      }
      featureReviewA = ticket "feature-review · RECIPE-10" {
        description '''
```
title:    the three questions over feature/add-recipe
label:    feature-review       → AGENT → review         (respawn: true)
category: review
parent:   RECIPE-5
metadata: { "branch": "feature/add-recipe" }
depends:  blocked-by RECIPE-9 — no review before the suite
skill:    pr-comments
```
**Brief:** three questions from first principles, findings as PR comments. Then the human verifies and closes
gate RECIPE-5, and that PR merges `feature/add-recipe` into `epic/recipe-box`.
'''
      }
    }

    featB = gate "Feature gate — Search the Box · RECIPE-11" {
      description '''
```
type:     gate
branch:   feature/search-recipes
pr:       https://github.com/…/pull/3
waits on: RECIPE-16 (feature-review)
PR base:  epic/recipe-box
```
Runs **side by side** with Add a Recipe: two features in parallel, each alone on its own branch.
'''
      backendB = ticket "backend-logic · RECIPE-12" {
        description '''
```
title:    searchRecipes — pure filter over the repository port
label:    backend-logic        → AGENT → engineer
category: backend-logic
parent:   RECIPE-11
metadata: { "branch": "feature/search-recipes" }
depends:  none — with RECIPE-6 it is the frontier
```
**Requirements**
1. `searchRecipes(query, records): Recipe[]` — pure, deterministic order
2. empty query returns all, newest first; whitespace trimmed
3. no match returns `[]`, never an error

**Files:** `src/recipes/search-recipes.ts`, `src/recipes/ports.ts` (read side)
'''
      }
      unitB = ticket "unit-tests · RECIPE-13" {
        description '''
```
title:    exhaustive coverage for searchRecipes
label:    unit-tests           → AGENT → tester-unit    (respawn: true)
category: unit-tests
parent:   RECIPE-11
metadata: { "branch": "feature/search-recipes" }
depends:  blocked-by RECIPE-12
skill:    framework-jest
```
**Cover:** empty query, whitespace, unicode titles, an empty store, tie-break ordering, case folding.
'''
      }
      frontendB = ticket "frontend-ui · RECIPE-14" {
        description '''
```
title:    SearchBar + live results list
label:    frontend-ui          → AGENT → frontend
category: frontend-ux
parent:   RECIPE-11
metadata: { "branch": "feature/search-recipes" }
depends:  blocked-by RECIPE-13
```
**Brief:** debounce, empty-state copy, highlighted matches, all from the shared component system.
'''
      }
      featureTestB = ticket "feature-test · RECIPE-15" {
        description '''
```
title:    the search pieces are wired
label:    feature-test         → AGENT → tester-feature (respawn: true)
category: feature-test
parent:   RECIPE-11
metadata: { "branch": "feature/search-recipes" }
depends:  blocked-by RECIPE-14 — the LAST task
skill:    framework-playwright
```
'''
      }
      featureReviewB = ticket "feature-review · RECIPE-16" {
        description '''
```
title:    the three questions over feature/search-recipes
label:    feature-review       → AGENT → review         (respawn: true)
category: review
parent:   RECIPE-11
metadata: { "branch": "feature/search-recipes" }
depends:  blocked-by RECIPE-15
skill:    pr-comments
```
'''
      }
    }
  }

  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // THE ALARMS — every reason string in the engine and both hooks, and what
  // happens to the ticket when it fires.
  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  alarms = layer "Alarms — every reason= in the code" {

    tickAlarms = layer "raised by the tick" {
      startupAbort = alarm "startup: the run refuses to start" {
        #fatal
        description '''
Before the first tick, each one prints to stderr and `exit 1`s:

| Check | Message |
|---|---|
| `bd jq claude git` on PATH | `beads-loop: <tool> not found on PATH` |
| the three numeric knobs | `BO_<VAR>=<x> is not a number` |
| the poll interval | `BO_POLL_S=<x> is unusable — it must divide 60 (1 2 3 4 5 6 10 12 15 20 30 60)` |
| a beads DB from `$PWD` | `no beads database found from $PWD — cd into the tower project and re-run` |
| the log dir | `cannot create <dir>` |

It is a real probe of "can we do anything here", not a check for a `.beads` folder — `bd` walks **up** from
the cwd and ignores `BEADS_DIR`. `bo` runs the same DB probe before it even starts the engine.
'''
      }
      bdFailure = alarm "ERROR reason=bd-failure exit=N" {
        description '''
`bd ready --claim` exited non-zero — the last line of `$logDir/bd-stderr` is quoted in `msg=`. Also raised
when the claim exits 0 but hands back no `id`, with the first 60 characters of the payload in `msg=`. The tick
returns; there is no claim to keep or return.
'''
      }
      labelAmbiguous = alarm "ERROR reason=label-ambiguous" {
        #leftClaimed
        description '''
Zero **or two** dispatching labels on a claim: `matched="…" labels="…"`. The dispatcher never guesses a
specialist. The ticket is LEFT CLAIMED so its 5-minute lease parks it off the frontier — otherwise the loop
would spin on the same unworkable ticket every tick — and the errors file is the alarm.
'''
      }
      metadataMissing = alarm "ERROR reason=metadata-missing" {
        #leftClaimed
        description '''
No `branch` in the claim's `metadata` — a plan agent skipped its one key. With no branch there is no folder to
work in and nothing to push, so it is a plan bug and gets exactly the treatment of a mislabelled ticket: left
claimed. The loop never invents a branch.
'''
      }
      branchBusyAlarm = alarm "CLAIMS reason=branch-busy unclaimed" {
        #unclaimed
        description '''
The branch this ticket wants is already checked out by a worker: `bd unclaim`, remember the ticket id in
`skippedTicket`, try the next claim — at most `BO_MAX_PARALLEL + 2` attempts a tick, then the waiting line
`branch_busy held=N`. One pair of hands per branch.
'''
      }
      worktreeFailed = alarm "ERROR reason=worktree-failed" {
        #leftClaimed
        description '''
`ensureWorktree` could not build the folder — none of `origin/<branch>`, `origin/<prTarget>`, `<prTarget>`,
`HEAD` resolved, or `git worktree add` failed. Left claimed, like a mislabelled ticket: a worker spawned into a
missing folder burns a slot on nothing.
'''
      }
      workerNotDispatched = alarm "ERROR reason=worker-not-dispatched" {
        #unclaimed
        description '''
`claude --bg` came back non-zero; the last line of that worker's `spawn-<ticket>` file is quoted in `msg=`.
`bd unclaim` — unlike every other tick alarm, this one puts the ticket **straight back**, because nothing was
ever spawned to work it and the next tick may well succeed.
'''
      }
      epicPrFailed = alarm "ERROR reason=epic-pr-failed step=…" {
        #leftClaimed
        description '''
The epic branch or its PR could not be published — and every feature PR below it aims at that branch, so nothing
beneath the epic has a base until this succeeds. Seven steps name exactly where it died:

| step | when |
|---|---|
| `walk` | `epicGateOf` could not reach a top of the parent chain in 4 hops |
| `gate-branch` | the epic gate carries no `branch` metadata of its own |
| `forge` | `detectForge` settled on nothing |
| `missing-tool` | the settled forge's CLI (`gh`/`glab`) is not installed |
| `push` | the epic branch could not be published off `main` |
| `pr` | no URL matching `/(pull\|merge_requests)/[0-9]+` came back |
| `annotate` | `bd update --set-metadata pr=` refused |

Left claimed every time: a worker spawned under an epic branch that does not exist burns a slot and files a
commit nobody can pull. The one place the epic gate's own branch appears is `gateBranch=` on the line — never
`branch=`, which belongs to the claim, and the two differ.
'''
      }
    }

    stopAlarms = layer "raised by the Stop hook — fail-closed" {
      missingToolHook = alarm "ERROR reason=missing-tool tool=<t>" {
        #staysClaimed
        description '''
`bd`, `jq` or `git` missing (checked up front), or the settled forge CLI missing. Note this alarm fires
**before** `BO_TICKET` is even checked for the tools case — a session the loop never spawned still exits on
line one.
'''
      }
      ticketNotInWork = alarm "ERROR reason=ticket-not-in-work state=<s>" {
        #staysClaimed
        description '''
The ticket is not `in_progress`. Two readings: state `closed` means this is a second Stop in a session that
already shipped, and the hook leaves **silently** rather than committing onto a shared branch a second time;
anything else (`not-found`, `open`) is an alarm.
'''
      }
      noBranchHook = alarm "ERROR reason=no-branch" {
        #staysClaimed
        description '''
`BO_BRANCH` was not on the spawn line — so the loop never got as far as reading the claim's `branch` metadata.
'''
      }
      noPrTarget = alarm "ERROR reason=no-pr-target" {
        #staysClaimed
        description '''
`BO_PR_TARGET` was not on the spawn. Named for the target, since `pr` now means the url: the loop reads the
base off the ticket's epic gate and says `main` when the ticket sits under the epic itself, so an absent target
means the hook is aiming blind and refuses to.
'''
      }
      notDeclaredDone = alarm "ERROR reason=not-declared-done" {
        #staysClaimed
        description '''
**The gate.** `$BO_DONE_FILE` does not exist: the session ended without saying, out loud, in the one place both
programs read, that its brief is finished. The session ending is not that statement — the marker is.

No commit, no push, no PR, no close. The ticket stays claimed so its lease parks it off the frontier and every
task chained behind it waits. That is the whole safety of a shared branch: a half-finished task cannot slide its
neighbours sideways. And the converse is the one failure this model cannot undo on its own — a worker that touches
the marker early files half a task onto a branch its siblings are queued behind.
'''
      }
      wrongBranch = alarm "ERROR reason=wrong-branch want=<b> got=<d>" {
        #staysClaimed
        description '''
The worker was told never to change branches; if it did anyway, this is the last place that can refuse to file
its work under the wrong ticket's name. `git branch --show-current` must equal `BO_BRANCH` exactly — a detached
tree reads as `got="detached"`.
'''
      }
      noTitle = alarm "ERROR reason=ticket-has-no-title" {
        #staysClaimed
        description '''
The commit message IS the ticket title (`bd show --json --long | .title`), so a ticket with no title cannot be
filed — the commit would be anonymous on a shared branch.
'''
      }
      commitFailed = alarm "ERROR reason=commit-failed" {
        #staysClaimed
        description '''
`git add -A` then `git commit -F -`, message = the ticket title plus a `Ticket: <id>` trailer. A review or test
ticket may legitimately land nothing (`diff --cached --quiet` skips the commit entirely), so this is a genuine
commit failure, not an empty tree.
'''
      }
      pushFailed = alarm "ERROR reason=push-failed" {
        #staysClaimed
        description '''
`git push -u origin <BO_BRANCH>` failed; the first 160 characters of its stderr are the `msg=`.
'''
      }
      forgeUnknown = alarm "ERROR reason=forge-unknown remote=<url>" {
        #staysClaimed
        description '''
All three settle steps came up empty — the remote name lied, it publishes neither `refs/pull/` nor
`refs/merge-requests/`, and the machine holds both `gh` and `glab`, or neither. The hook refuses to guess a forge.
'''
      }
      prNotCreated = alarm "ERROR reason=pr-not-created forge=<f>" {
        #staysClaimed
        description '''
Nothing came back that looks like a PR url. Either the list of the branch's existing PRs and the create both
failed, or the CLI printed an error quoting some other URL — only `/(pull|merge_requests)/<digits>` counts.
'''
      }
      ticketNotAnnotated = alarm "ERROR reason=ticket-not-annotated pr=<url>" {
        #staysClaimed
        description '''
`bd update --set-metadata pr=<url>` failed. The work is pushed but the ticket cannot say where it went, so the
hook stops rather than closing a ticket whose PR is unfindable. (`--set-metadata` **merges**, so this never
disturbs the plan's `branch` key.)
'''
      }
      closeFailed = alarm "ERROR reason=close-failed pr=<url>" {
        #staysClaimed
        description '''
`bd close <ticket> --session <session_id> --reason "worker session … finished; PR …"` failed. The last step, and
the only one where everything else already succeeded — the commit, the push and the PR are all out there with no
record in the tower. `bd` refuses to close a ticket whose assignee is not the actor, and the dispatcher claimed
this one, so the hook deliberately passes the real `session_id` rather than overriding the actor.
'''
      }
    }

    failAlarms = layer "raised by the StopFailure hook" {
      ticketNotFoundFail = alarm "ERROR reason=ticket-not-found" {
        description '''
`bd show --json` for `BO_TICKET` returned no state at all. Anything not `in_progress` (already parked, already
shipped) is somebody else's business and the hook leaves silently — a dead session must not rewrite a ticket
another worker owns.
'''
      }
      failNotAnnotated = alarm "ERROR reason=ticket-not-annotated kind=<k>" {
        #staysClaimed
        description '''
The `--append-notes` writing the reason failed, so the hook stops: a ticket parked in failure with nothing said
about why is worse than one left `in_progress`, so the reason goes on **first**.
'''
      }
      statusNotSet = alarm "ERROR reason=status-not-set kind=<k> from=<state>" {
        #staysClaimed
        description '''
`bd update --status failed-agent` refused, so the ticket did NOT get out of `bd reclaim`'s path and will be swept
back to `open` when the lease lapses. The `failures` line never gets written, which is the tell.
'''
      }
    }
  }

  waiting = layer "Waiting lines — nothing failed, nothing ran (one a minute per reason)" {
    frontierEmpty = waitingLine "reason=frontier_empty" {
      #idle
      description '''
The claim came back empty and the stall probe found **no** off-frontier work: the tower is finished, or nobody has
planned anything yet. This is the calm one.
'''
    }
    towerStalled = waitingLine "reason=tower_stalled waiting=N" {
      #idle
      description '''
The other half of an empty frontier, and the two need different alarms: work tickets **do** exist
(`bd list --status open,blocked --limit 0` counted N of them) but an active blocker holds them off `bd ready` —
an open gate two levels below, most often, which **only a human** may close. `bd list` hides closed tickets and
gates by default, so N counts work tickets only. A broken tower must never read as a calm one.
'''
    }
    branchBusyWaiting = waitingLine "reason=branch_busy held=N" {
      #idle
      description '''
Tickets were claimable and every one of them wanted a branch already at work: N were unclaimed and skipped this
tick. The graph makes a feature sequential, so this is the fallback for a tower that was never chained.
'''
    }
    maxParallel = waitingLine "reason=max_parallel in_progress=N" {
      #idle
      description '''
`BO_MAX_PARALLEL` background sessions are already alive and N tickets are in progress — the workers are working,
so the tick simply does not claim.
'''
    }
    maxParallelStalled = waitingLine "reason=max_parallel_stalled in_progress=0" {
      #idle
      description '''
At capacity with **nothing in progress**: the slots are ghosts — sessions counted busy whose tickets are gone. The
count comes fresh from `claude agents --json` every tick precisely so this state is visible rather than silent.
'''
    }
  }

  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // THE EDGES
  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  // the front door
  human -> boCli "bo loop, from inside the tower"
  boCli -[execs]-> dispatcher "bash, in the foreground"
  boCli -> beads "refuses to start without a DB"
  dispatcher -[calls]-> knobs "once, at startup"

  // what the tick shells out to
  dispatcher -[calls]-> beads "bd ready --claim --json, the only atomic claim"
  dispatcher -[calls]-> beads "bd unclaim / bd list --status in_progress / bd show --json"
  dispatcher -[calls]-> claudeCli "claude agents --json — the capacity count"
  dispatcher -[calls]-> claudeCli "claude --bg --agent <agent> --permission-mode bypassPermissions"
  dispatcher -[calls]-> gitCli "git worktree add -B <branch> <path> <start>"
  dispatcher -[calls]-> forgeCli "ensureEpicGatePr: list the PR, else create it"
  dispatcher -> forge "push epic/<slug> off main, open its PR into main"
  dispatcher -> agentMap "one claim, exactly one matching key"

  // the log
  dispatcher -[writesLog]-> logRun "claims · waiting · results"
  dispatcher -[writesLog]-> logRun.fClaims "reason=dispatched, and every unclaim"
  dispatcher -[writesLog]-> logRun.fWaiting "one a minute per reason"
  dispatcher -[writesLog]-> logRun.fErrors "the tick's own alarms, never rate-limited"
  dispatcher -[writesLog]-> logRun.fResults "ticks=N claimed=M at the end of the run"
  dispatcher -[writesLog]-> logRun.fSpawn "the spawn shell redirects >\"$spawnFile\" 2>&1"
  dispatcher -[readsLog]-> logRun.fSpawn "sessionFromSpawn — the session id lives nowhere else"
  dispatcher -> lineDef "sourced: one line format"
  stopHook -[writesLog]-> logRun.fCloses "url=<pr>"
  stopHook -[writesLog]-> logRun.fErrors "the hook's own alarms"
  stopHook -> lineDef "sourced, with BO_LOG_QUIET=1"
  failHook -[writesLog]-> logRun.fFailures "from=… kind=… msg=…"
  failHook -[writesLog]-> logRun.fErrors "the hook's own alarms"
  failHook -> lineDef "sourced, with BO_LOG_QUIET=1"

  // the spawn contract
  dispatcher -> spawnEnv "sets eight BO_* vars on the spawn line"
  stopHook -> spawnEnv "reads them; BO_TICKET is the only test that this is a dispatched worker"
  failHook -> spawnEnv "reads them"
  hooksJson -> stopHook "registers Stop, timeout 300"
  hooksJson -> failHook "registers StopFailure, timeout 60"

  // the workers and their tools
  workers.engineer -> beads "bd show --json <ticket> — the ticket IS the brief"
  workers.engineer -> beads "bd heartbeat <ticket> — the 5-minute lease must never lapse"
  workers.engineer -> logRun.fDone "touch $BO_DONE_FILE — last action, only if the brief is genuinely complete"
  workers.engineer -> forge "commit + push happen via the Stop hook, never from the worker"
  workers.engineer -> stopHook "the harness fires Stop when the session ends, declared or not"
  workers.engineer -> failHook "the harness fires StopFailure when the session dies mid-turn"
  human -> planAgents.planEpic "the vague idea, and the answers to its questions"
  human -> planAgents.planFeature "the epic id, or 'there is no epic'"
  human -> planAgents.planTask "the one feature to fragment"
  human -> beads "bd show the ticket, its comments and the PR, before closing anything"
  claudeCli -> workers.engineer "runs the background session, bypassPermissions, unattended"
  planAgents.planEpic -> beads "bd create -t gate, bd config set types.custom"
  planAgents.planFeature -> beads "bd create -t gate --parent <epic>"
  planAgents.planTask -> beads "bd create -l <label> --parent <feature>, then the bd show --json round trip"

  // the Stop and StopFailure hooks against the world
  stopHook -[calls]-> beads "bd show --json --long, bd update --set-metadata pr=…, bd close --session <id>"
  stopHook -[calls]-> gitCli "add -A, commit, push -u origin <branch>"
  stopHook -[calls]-> forgeCli "the PR already on this branch, else create it"
  stopHook -> forge "one PR per branch, forever"
  failHook -[calls]-> beads "bd update --append-notes …, bd update --status failed-agent"
  failHook -> beads "takes the dead ticket out of bd reclaim's path"

  // the AGENT map: one edge per dispatching label — that label alone decides the worker
  dispatcher -[dispatches]-> workers.engineer "backend-logic"
  dispatcher -[dispatches]-> workers.testerUnit "unit-tests"
  dispatcher -[dispatches]-> workers.frontend "frontend-ui"
  dispatcher -[dispatches]-> workers.testerFeature "feature-test"
  dispatcher -[dispatches]-> workers.testerE2e "epic-e2e-test"
  dispatcher -[dispatches]-> workers.reviewAgent "feature-review"
  dispatcher -[dispatches]-> workers.reviewAgent "epic-review"
  dispatcher -[dispatches]-> workers.mergeAgent "epic-merge-pr"

  // each worker points at the tickets its label owns
  workers.engineer -[works]-> tower.featA.backendA
  workers.engineer -[works]-> tower.featB.backendB
  workers.testerUnit -[works]-> tower.featA.unitA
  workers.testerUnit -[works]-> tower.featB.unitB
  workers.frontend -[works]-> tower.featA.frontendA
  workers.frontend -[works]-> tower.featB.frontendB
  workers.testerFeature -[works]-> tower.featA.featureTestA
  workers.testerFeature -[works]-> tower.featB.featureTestB
  workers.testerE2e -[works]-> tower.epic.epicE2e
  workers.reviewAgent -[works]-> tower.featA.featureReviewA
  workers.reviewAgent -[works]-> tower.featB.featureReviewB
  workers.reviewAgent -[works]-> tower.epic.epicReview
  workers.mergeAgent -[works]-> tower.epic.epicMergePr

  // the plan agents build the tower top-down, each writing only its own tier's branch
  planAgents.planEpic -[plans]-> tower.epic "one epic gate + merge / e2e / review, branch epic/recipe-box"
  planAgents.planFeature -[plans]-> tower.featA "flow-sized features, branch feature/add-recipe"
  planAgents.planFeature -[plans]-> tower.featB "flow-sized features, branch feature/search-recipes"
  planAgents.planTask -[plans]-> tower.featA.backendA "the tasks of ONE feature, chained one to the next"
  planAgents.planTask -[plans]-> tower.featB.backendB "the tasks of ONE feature, chained one to the next"

  // only a human closes a gate
  human -[closes]-> tower.epic "after epic-review, thumbs-up on the PR into main"
  human -[closes]-> tower.featA "after feature-review; its PR merges into epic/recipe-box"
  human -[closes]-> tower.featB "after feature-review"

  // the depends-on chain — inside each feature it is ONE linked line, never a fan
  tower.featA.backendA -[blocks]-> tower.featA.unitA
  tower.featA.unitA -[blocks]-> tower.featA.frontendA
  tower.featA.frontendA -[blocks]-> tower.featA.featureTestA
  tower.featA.featureTestA -[blocks]-> tower.featA.featureReviewA

  tower.featB.backendB -[blocks]-> tower.featB.unitB
  tower.featB.unitB -[blocks]-> tower.featB.frontendB
  tower.featB.frontendB -[blocks]-> tower.featB.featureTestB
  tower.featB.featureTestB -[blocks]-> tower.featB.featureReviewB

  tower.featA -[blocks]-> tower.epic.epicMergePr
  tower.featB -[blocks]-> tower.epic.epicMergePr
  tower.epic.epicMergePr -[blocks]-> tower.epic.epicE2e
  tower.epic.epicE2e -[blocks]-> tower.epic.epicReview

  // who raises what
  dispatcher -[raises]-> alarms.tickAlarms.startupAbort
  dispatcher -[raises]-> alarms.tickAlarms.bdFailure
  dispatcher -[raises]-> alarms.tickAlarms.labelAmbiguous
  dispatcher -[raises]-> alarms.tickAlarms.metadataMissing
  dispatcher -[raises]-> alarms.tickAlarms.branchBusyAlarm
  dispatcher -[raises]-> alarms.tickAlarms.worktreeFailed
  dispatcher -[raises]-> alarms.tickAlarms.workerNotDispatched
  dispatcher -[raises]-> alarms.tickAlarms.epicPrFailed

  stopHook -[raises]-> alarms.stopAlarms.missingToolHook
  stopHook -[raises]-> alarms.stopAlarms.ticketNotInWork
  stopHook -[raises]-> alarms.stopAlarms.noBranchHook
  stopHook -[raises]-> alarms.stopAlarms.noPrTarget
  stopHook -[raises]-> alarms.stopAlarms.notDeclaredDone
  stopHook -[raises]-> alarms.stopAlarms.wrongBranch
  stopHook -[raises]-> alarms.stopAlarms.noTitle
  stopHook -[raises]-> alarms.stopAlarms.commitFailed
  stopHook -[raises]-> alarms.stopAlarms.pushFailed
  stopHook -[raises]-> alarms.stopAlarms.forgeUnknown
  stopHook -[raises]-> alarms.stopAlarms.prNotCreated
  stopHook -[raises]-> alarms.stopAlarms.ticketNotAnnotated
  stopHook -[raises]-> alarms.stopAlarms.closeFailed

  failHook -[raises]-> alarms.failAlarms.ticketNotFoundFail
  failHook -[raises]-> alarms.failAlarms.failNotAnnotated
  failHook -[raises]-> alarms.failAlarms.statusNotSet

  // who writes which waiting line
  dispatcher -[probes]-> waiting.frontierEmpty
  dispatcher -[probes]-> waiting.towerStalled
  dispatcher -[probes]-> waiting.branchBusyWaiting
  dispatcher -[probes]-> waiting.maxParallel
  dispatcher -[probes]-> waiting.maxParallelStalled
}
